A||||25||mrfixit58||Cradle Suggestion for V-8 Installation||||3||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||05-30-2000||09:39 AM||||I've been doing some research on V-8 conversions and listing to comments by other members and I think I may have a suggestion to help the torque steer problem.<P>I seems that all the kits I've seen use solid mounts from the motor to the cradle and leave the rubber cradle to space frame mounts (except for 88s). While O.K. for vibration dampening, this setup may be contributing to the torque steer problem. <P>I've replaced a few motor mounts in old hot rods and know that the force applied to these mounts with a V-8 can be substantial. If you mount the motor solid to the cradle, this leaves the cradle bushings as the only weak point and will have to move with the motor torque. Consequently, if the cradle moves, this will substantially change the geometry of the suspension.<P>On the flip side, if you used rubber engine mounts and solid cradle mounts, the cradle would better stay put during acceleration (at least until you twist the cradle itself). True?<P>Unfortunately, I haven't seen any kits offered with rubber motor mounts. Has anyone else?<P>Roy||||reg||3
Z||000001||pollock||05-30-2000||10:24 AM||||Good point.  <P>I haven't seen any rubber-mount kits either, but i'm sure it could be done.  the way i would approach it is with machined polyurethane bushings.  You can buy poly-u as a raw material from industrial suppliers and cut it to the shape you want.  ||||reg||10
Z||000002||batboy||05-30-2000||12:26 PM||||Makes sense to me. Fabricating your own poly motor mounts might be a viable option, especially if you're in the process of doing the swap. Since my V8 swap is already done, I probably won't do anything drastic until I replace the engine. But, I have a plan how to mount a second dogbone and will probably replace the cradle bushings with poly. If and when I do this, I'll report how it works.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||05-30-2000||01:34 PM||||Please do, Batboy. But, be careful. Unless you like feeling every vibration, the poly bushings may be too hard. Unless your motor is VERY well balanced, it may rattle your teeth loose.<P>My thought was to mount the motor on rubber motor mounts like those used in a 57 Chevy.<BR>Basically, the mounts are a hunk of rubber with a mounting stud out each end. As a precaution, I thought a safety buckle made from aircraft cable, bolted from the block to the cradle. That way, you would be able to isolate the vibrations on all but heavy pedal accelerations. This method used in the early muscle cars using a few links of chain.<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||05-30-2000||02:40 PM||||I chained down the motor mounts in my old Chevelle back in the 70's. Streetrodders also use that style of small block Chevy motor mounts with their engine swapping conversion kits. If I dug around in my shed, I'd find a pair of those motor mounts that I was going to use on my 40 Chevy Coupe before I changed the entire chassis instead.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sage||05-30-2000||03:37 PM||||Sounds like an excellant idea to me.  Used to run a 12sec.(1/4mi)full bodied 57 chevy with a 60over 283 and we used mounts that were  a harder rubber than stock, but would still "give" a little.  Then we chained the engine down to the frame!  Ran separate air lines to the air shocks and preloaded one side to compensate for torque twisting.  Worked very well.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-31-2000||02:25 PM||||I agree. there are some comments about exactly this that I've made in other threads.<P>I'd definatly use rubber. Preferably of the internally braced kind (Most TRW moounts are internally braced. No need for chain most times.)<P>Unless it's a space issue, I don't see why you couldn't make brackets on the cradle that would bolt to nearly any common V8 mount. I can't see why it should be a huge space problem, the V8 shouldn't be much wider than the V6. I can see where it might make the engine sit higher, that could be a little dificult to work out.<P>Most V8 mounts I'v seen bolt to the block direct on top and have and inverted U shaped bottom. A single bolt passes thru the U and the lower bracket. I'd guess the hight as I remember it would be about 2-3 inches minimum for that type mount, not including any extra hieght in the lower bracket.<P>The only thing I can think of why you couldn't do it pretty easy is the extra hieght. It seems likely that people are doing the direct mount to avoid altering the craddle to accept proper mounts. There's no reason you can't alter the craddle as long as you preserve the overall geometery, and make good strong cuts and gussets.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||05-31-2000||05:51 PM||||Ogre, you make some good points. The engine height on these V8 conversions is important. As it is, you can barely get one of those trianglar Edelbrock air cleaners on and just about nothing else will fit. I've seen a host of different methods to swap engines into antique cars. Some methods work better than others. There is not just one right way.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||05-31-2000||06:05 PM||||I remember from V8 Vega swaps that the mid-seventies Chevy truck mounts are the slimmest rubber mounts around,that are pretty firm also.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Spyder||05-31-2000||06:24 PM||||My engine’s front mount is welded solid to the engine cradle frame.  The transaxle still uses the rubber mounts.  And the cradle mounts I replaced with just new stock rubber mounts.  But I do have a “Torque Rod” connected from the exhaust manifold to the engine cradle frame for the torque.  I’m not using the dog bone.  I was going to use the solid (aluminum) engine cradle mounts but I was afraid of having it too solidly mounted, other words, to keep it from breaking something.<BR>The suspension has not been set up right so I can’t vouch for the feel.||||reg||1
Z||000010||pherder||05-31-2000||06:30 PM||||Guys,<P>One word ... axle.<P>It goes right where the "stock" V8 eng. mounts go.<P>I tried several different ways to use the "stock" V8 mounting bosses on the "rear" side of the engine and only had some success with a system on an early (84-87) cradle.  The 88 cradle is different enough that that method will not work.<P>BTW, the 88 cradle does not have ANY bushings, it is solid metal mounts.  The rear is just a "flat" plate that the mounting bolt goes up thru to the "captured" nut in the frame.  The front is a tube that is welded to the cradle that the front bolt passes thru to secure the front of the cradle.  <P>V8 Archie uses the same solid welded mount on the 88s as he does on the earlier model Fieros.  The install video he supplies has an 88 Fiero as it's subject.<P>AFTER getting the HO Quad 4 Coupe and the HO 3.1 GT running, my next project will be lightening up the 88 Formula (i.e. like George Ryan's Quad 4 race car) and putting in a warmed over 350 with a SuperRam TPI.<P>It will be dragged raced and autocrossed but will remain "street legal" IAW Kansas laws (which aren't much ... NO inspections!)<P>The local SCCA club has a Street Modified class that they allow just about anything to run in.  The national rules states that the Street classes must be a 4 seat vehicle but the local club allows a NSX to compete (and usually win) in the SM class.<P>Paul||||reg||3
Z||000011||theogre||06-01-2000||09:35 AM||||Forgot about the axle, but that still doesn't explain the requirement to weld the front. The axle is in back of the engine.<P>Even still you could make a bracket that would clear the axle in the back. I'm not sure just how off top of my head.<P>Another posibility would be to make a bracket that can use the 4 cyl engine mount location. between that and the tranny you'd get a 3 point mount. That along with a torque reaction strut to the cradle should keep it in place.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mrfixit58||06-01-2000||11:08 AM||||From what I've been able to find out, all the currently offered kits have the motor mounted using a plate on the front of the motor. In other words, the conventional side mounts are not used.<P>My thought was to use a front mount bracket like the one in the kits but, instead of bolting or welding it to the frame, modify the frame and mount to accept the rubber motor mounts. Having your own MIG welder has it's advantages.<P>A torque rod may still be required but I would probably use a rubber isolation mount or better yet, design it so you could press in a rubber A-arm bushing on at least one end.<P>I'm trying to dig up enough background to build my own kit which will require different axles (I want to move the transmission over two inches like the Zumalt kit). I still need a source for the transmission adapter and flywheel so any suggestions are appreciated.<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000013||HarryG||06-01-2000||01:06 PM||||Roy,<BR>Have you found stock-type axles which will fit when you move the eng/trans?  I have considered such a swap, only moving less than 2" to keep from cutting any sheetmetal.<BR>I want to try an intermediate axle to keep from using that expensive long axle on the passenger side. I haven't seen his car, but I've corresponded with a guy who built his own kit and used a rubber front mount.  He also used two dogbones on the rear, one from each head. His axles came from Mark Williams.  Centerforce makes 2 flywheels for SBC/Fiero trans applications, but they use standard hex head bolts, so the trans adapter has to be thicker.  <BR>You're probably on your own for the adapter;<BR>OR, you get might get lucky and find a retired machinist with a lathe and Bridgeport mill in his basement!<BR>Hope this helps!<BR>Harry ||||reg||3
Z||000014||fierov8||06-01-2000||02:28 PM||||Watch this space!<P>Finally (I'm lazy when it comes to taking pictures and getting them developed.  And<BR>NO digital camera -- rather put the $$$ <BR>into the car).<P>But I digress.....<P>Will have scanned pics tomorrow and will <BR>post to the forum and/or photopoint.<P>Pherder -- promised these for a while -- <BR>still want hardcopies?  They'll be in the<BR>mail........||||reg||10
Z||000015||mrfixit58||06-01-2000||05:32 PM||||Harry,<P>No, I haven't looked for stock axles. I just assumed that since kit manufacturers like Zumalt had them made that they were not available stock. Good tip on the axle manufacturer, do you have a number or site address?<P>As far as the adaptor, there is a company called Kennedy Engineering that used to advertize in all the kit car mags and they specialized in adaptors. I used to have their catalog a couple of years ago but I haven't seen their add recently. Hope they are still around. If not, well, a cardboard templete and a tourch and some 1/4 inch plate ought to get me started.<P>Any info you can get from anyone who has made their own kit would be helpful. This is the kind of research I did (word-of -mouth, pre-computers) when I changed out the 4-banger and installed a SBC in my 72 Chevy LUV truck. It still cost me about twice what I had estimated.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Roy||||reg||2
Z||000016||fierov8||06-02-2000||05:13 PM||||got the pics posted up to photopoint:<BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=220465&a=6619342" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=220465&a=6619342</A> <P>....or you can check out all the albums I<BR>have started to fill for the v8 project <BR>under my email ID:  fierov8@tensor.com<P>See -- it is possible to use standard SBC<BR>chevy mounts for a 350 in a fiero!  It <BR>just takes a little work......<P>Let me know what y'all think  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>-jeff d<BR>fort worth, TX||||reg||10
Z||000017||fierov8||06-02-2000||06:20 PM||||....and I know about the links being switched.  One will do crazy things to get wheels under car before moving to a new house.<P>They have since been swapped to their correct position.<P>-jeff d<BR>Fort Worth, TX<P>no, really, I _do_ have a manual and know what I am doing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000018||mrfixit58||06-02-2000||10:02 PM||||Hey Jeff,<P>I like it!! Have you placed your motor in yet and what have you got planned for the exhaust? Shorty headers? What about A/C? Are you keeping the stock location so you can use the stock axles? Sorry for all the questions but before it's over, I'll probably have many more.<P>Roy||||reg||14
Z||000019||fierov8||06-05-2000||09:52 AM||||Roy,<BR>  <BR>The motor has been "test-fit" in place<BR>once -- I was able to drop it in from <BR>the top with the tubular L98 manifolds<BR>on it!!!  There is room  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The exhaust will be a true dual setup --<BR>dual cats and dual flowmasters.  And<BR>I have no trunk space to speak of (cut<BR>it out and just placed a flat piece of<BR>aluminum over the opening).<P>I'll be using the cast iron ram's horn<BR>manifolds -- about the best/only way<BR>to go.<P>A/C is still in the picture -- I have to<BR>drive on the streets in TX  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The axles are Mark William's parts -- <BR>the engine has been shifted over 2" to the <BR>left.  I'm in the process of notching the<BR>left side frame rail right now.<P>-jeff d<BR>Fort Worth, TX||||reg||10
Z||000020||mrfixit58||06-05-2000||01:27 PM||||If the tubular manifolds fit, then why are you going back to the "rams horn" manifolds? Seems the tubular units would have better flow. Based on what you see, would a set of the "shorty, block hugger" headers fit? If not what do you see as the main interference obstacle(s)?<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000021||fierov8||06-06-2000||12:28 AM||||Roy,<P>I got the cradle and exhaust system from someone who originally built it for their Fiero and later decided to change up to a Northstar.  Hence, the exhaust system was originally built up around the ram's horns.  I may investigate using alternate exhaust systems once the car is up, running, and baselined on the existing exhaust system.  FWIW, I measured the ram's horns and the L98 tubular exhaust and the L98 tubes are just a little "longer" that the ram's horns.<P>Also -- block huggers won't do it -- they angle back in towards the block, which puts the exhaust exit right in line with the axles on the backside of the motor.  You need an exhaust that exits straight down.<P>I have heard that Sanderson makes a tubular exhaust/shorty header that will work (the CC90), but I'd need to get my mitts on a sample to measure and see how close they are to the ram's horns dimensionally.<P>-jeff d<BR>Fort Worth, TX  <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B>If the tubular manifolds fit, then why are you going back to the "rams horn" manifolds? Seems the tubular units would have better flow. Based on what you see, would a set of the "shorty, block hugger" headers fit? If not what do you see as the main interference obstacle(s)?<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||mrfixit58||06-06-2000||02:20 PM||||Here are a couple of headers made by Sanderson. Does it lok like any of these would work?<BR> <A HREF="http://www.sandersonheaders.com/pagesbypartnum/cc2.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sandersonheaders.com/pagesbypartnum/cc2.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.sandersonheaders.com/pagesbypartnum/cc3.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sandersonheaders.com/pagesbypartnum/cc3.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.sandersonheaders.com/pagesbypartnum/qp1000a.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sandersonheaders.com/pagesbypartnum/qp1000a.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.sandersonheaders.com/pagesbypartnum/qp1200.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sandersonheaders.com/pagesbypartnum/qp1200.htm</A> <P>The home address for Sanderson Headers is: <A HREF="http://www.sandersonheaders.com/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sandersonheaders.com/index.htm</A> <P>There is a lot of good info on headers and making good usuable power.<P>Roy<P>||||reg||3
Z||000023||pherder||06-06-2000||06:29 PM||||Here is a URL for the informationon the Sanderson CC90 headers ...<P> <A HREF="http://www.centuryperformance.com/sanderson20.htm#CC90" TARGET=_blank>www.centuryperformance.com/sanderson20.htm#CC90</A> <P>A picture ...<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.centuryperformance.com/Sanderson/sdhcc90.gif">   <P>Paul<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 06-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||mrfixit58||06-08-2000||11:06 AM||||How 'bout it Batboy, Can you take some measurements and let us know it you think the Sanderson headers will work?<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000025||batboy||06-08-2000||03:39 PM||||Sure, I want to install some type of tube headers when I put my new engine in, which probably won't be until fall or next spring at this point. If you look at streetrod shorty headers, there are several different styles to choose from including block hugging shorties that dump straight down. I like the look of the Sanderson headers. Paul has a V8 project too, maybe he's already researched the Sanderson headers. The ram's horn manifolds do flow better than the log style manifolds, I have them on my 40 Chevy coupe and on a truck, but the tube headers still flow much better.||||reg||1
